CONVENTIONAL MATHEMATICAL SPACES HIERARCHY
→ 111 → Metaphysics / Ontology

Topological space  (X, τ)
  defined only by a collection of open sets
  continuity, compactness, connectedness
  no distance, no size, no angles yet

        ↓ add a distance function

Metric space  (X, d)
  d : X × X → [0, ∞)
  d(x,y)=0 ⇔ x=y
  d(x,y)=d(y,x)
  d(x,z) ≤ d(x,y)+d(y,z)
  the metric induces the topology

        ↓ add vector operations

Vector / linear space  (V, +, · )
  αx + βy   (scalars α,β and vectors x,y)
  0 vector, additive inverses
  still no notion of length or angle

        ↓ add a size / add angles

Normed space          (V, ‖·‖)          Inner-product space     (V, ⟨·,·⟩)
‖x‖ ≥ 0                                 ⟨x,x⟩ ≥ 0
‖x‖=0 ⇔ x=0                             ⟨x,x⟩=0 ⇔ x=0
‖αx‖ = |α| ‖x‖                          linear in first argument
triangle inequality                     conjugate-symmetric
induces metric                          induces the norm
d(x,y)=‖x-y‖                            ‖x‖ = √⟨x,x⟩
                                        also gives angles via
                                        cos θ = ⟨x,y⟩ / (‖x‖‖y‖)

Complete versions
Banach space  = complete normed space
Hilbert space = complete inner-product space

        ↓ finite-dimensional + standard structure

Euclidean space  ℝⁿ
  vectors (x₁,…,xₙ)
  standard inner product  ⟨x,y⟩ = Σ xᵢ yᵢ
  Euclidean norm          ‖x‖₂ = √(Σ xᵢ²)
  the familiar geometry of everyday space
